Project conventions win. Read the neighbouring files first.

## Layering

routes/<domain>/ → services/<domain>/{<domain>_service.ts (factory + contract interface),
<domain>_client.ts (transport), <domain>_service.mock.ts, errors.ts, types.ts, repositories/}

❌ export class MaintenanceService { constructor() {} }
✅ export interface IMaintenanceService {
     /** Reads the persisted state. */
     getState(): Promise<TMaintenanceState>;
   }
   export function createMaintenanceService(deps: IDeps): IMaintenanceService {}
Factory plus a JSDoc'd contract interface. Classes only for errors, or where a framework requires one.

A service earns its own module when it holds state or exposes three or more operations. Below that,
export a plain function.

## Validation

❌ query: z.object({ name: z.string() })
✅ query: z.object({ name: z.string().max(MAX_ID_LENGTH).describe('The name of the stream.') })
Validate where untrusted data enters. Every string and array carries an explicit bound, because
unbounded input is a denial-of-service vector. Every field carries a description.

## Errors

❌ catch (e)                               ❌ throw new Error('Feature not enabled')
✅ catch (error)
✅ export class FeatureNotEnabledError extends StatusError {
     static DEFAULT_MESSAGE = 'Feature is not enabled.';
     constructor(message = FeatureNotEnabledError.DEFAULT_MESSAGE) {
       super(message, 403);
       this.name = 'FeatureNotEnabledError';
     }
   }
Named `<Domain><Condition>Error`, carrying a status code.

❌ const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
✅ const message = getFormattedError(error);
One normalisation helper per project, falling back to 'Unknown error'.

Throw typed domain errors inside a single request path; the route layer maps them to status codes.
An operation that fans out over many targets collects instead of throwing:

✅ const failures: IFailure[] = [];
   for (const target of targets) {
     // sequential so a failure can be attributed to its target
   }
   return { state, partialFailures: failures };

## Async

❌ client.fetch(endpoint).then((result) => { setData(result); });
✅ const result = await client.fetch(endpoint);
✅ client.fetch(endpoint).then(setData);        // point-free chain onto an existing function

✅ await Promise.all(checks.map(async (check) => ({ check, error: await run(check) })));
Sequential await only when order matters or per-item failures must be attributed. Say which in a
comment. Thread an abort signal through long-running or user-cancellable requests, not every call.
